// The depth guard, pinned by a value that TERMINATES either way -- unlike
// `json_stringify_cycle.nova`'s cyclic value, which does not.
//
// That cyclic fixture is what the guard exists for, but it makes a poor
// mutation detector: deleting the guard does not make the cyclic case fail
// fast by a different message, it makes it never finish, because an
// unguarded cyclic value has no natural floor at all. A test that discovers
// that by hanging past a suite timeout is worse than one that discovers it
// promptly.
//
// This value has no cycle -- it is `MAX_RENDER_DEPTH + 1` = 100001 nested
// arrays around a leaf, built by loop the same way `json_render_deep.nova`
// is, just one level past the guard's bound instead of far below it. With
// the guard in place this panics; with the guard deleted this RENDERS AND
// EXITS 0, because there is nothing left to stop it and the value is finite.
// That is what turns a deleted guard into a prompt, named test failure
// instead of a hang: the `.failure()` assertion below fails immediately,
// by exit code, the moment the guard is gone.
//
// Measured against this build, with the guard in place: this panics
// `stringify: nesting too deep or cyclic value` in about 2 seconds. With
// the guard deleted, this value still terminates on its own, because it is
// finite: it renders and exits 0 rather than hanging the way a cyclic value
// does once its guard is gone, and 100001 nested arrays around a leaf render
// to `2 * 100001 + 1 == 200003` characters by the same arithmetic
// `json_render_deep.stdout` and `json_render_guard_under.stdout` both pin.
fn main() {
    let mut v = Number(1.0)
    let mut i = 0
    while i < 100001 {
        v = Array([v])
        i = i + 1
    }
    println("built, about to render")
    println("${stringify(v).len()}")
}
